%I A398336 #11 Sep 01 2026 13:24:42
%S A398336 1,2,4,5,8,10,16,20,32,40,64,128,256,512,1024
%N A398336 Numbers m coprime to 3 such that, for k >= 0, sopfr(3^k*m) <= sopfr(j) for j > 3^k*m, where sopfr is A001414; numbers dividing 40 or 1024.
%C A398336 The set of numbers given by 3^k*m in the definition is the full set of numbers with the specified property. The significance of 3 here is essentially described by _Richard R. Forberg_'s 2014 comment in A001113.
%C A398336 It is simple to show that if a number k is not a term then no multiples of k are in the sequence.
%e A398336 The bounding cases that constrain the sequence to be finite are as follows. In each case it suffices to compare with the next larger power of 3:
%e A398336 - for primes greater than 5, like this: sopfr(7) = 7 > 6 = sopfr(9), so 7 is not in the sequence; therefore all multiples of 7 are absent since sopfr(7k) = 7 + sopfr(k) > 6 + sopfr(k) = sopfr(9k).
%e A398336 - sopfr(25) = 10 > 9 = sopfr(27), so 25 is not in the sequence.
%e A398336 - sopfr(80) = 13 > 12 = sopfr(81), so 80 is not in the sequence.
%e A398336 - sopfr(2048) = 22 > 21 = sopfr(2187), so 2048 is not in the sequence.
%Y A398336 Cf. A000244, A000792, A001113, A001414.
%K A398336 nonn,easy,fini,full,new
%O A398336 1,2
%A A398336 _Peter Munn_, Sep 01 2026
